  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Clouds and more seasonable temperatures return \nfor the weekend into early next week. \n\nA large surface high has moved into the area from the Upper \nMidwest, while an upper-level ridge is across the Southeastern \nUS. This combination is keeping mostly clear skies and well \nabove normal temperatures across the area this evening. A dry \ncold front moved through earlier this afternoon. Behind the \nfront, much drier air has moved in, with dew points having\nfallen into the 30s. \n\nFor Saturday, temperatures will return to around normal for \nthis time of year, with highs in the 60s/70s. Clouds will also \nincrease by tomorrow afternoon, as an inverted trough forms \nalong the Ohio River Valley. Halloween night will be dry and \nseasonable, though heavy cloud cover and light winds will add to\nthe spookiness. \n\nHigh pressure will \"wedge\" in east of the Appalachians this\nweekend, but temperatures will still remain around normal due \nto the flow coming off of the Atlantic Ocean rather than from the\nNortheast. The clouds stick around through the weekend and into\nearly next week, with temperatures remaining slightly above \nnormal. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Next chance of rain will be mid to late next week.\n\nThe dry weather is expected to continue through at least \nTuesday, which would be the 17th consecutive day with no \nrainfall across most of the area. While that is nowhere near a \nrecord, it is noteworthy. The system that was expected to impact\nour area early next week is expected to remain off to our \nsouth. Rain chances slightly increase on Wednesday, with a better\nchance on Thursday. \n\nA large upper-level trough will dive down across the central \nCONUS, and move east into our area. Models are beginning to hint\nat the system weakening as it arrives, though confidence this \nfar out remains low. In addition, the synoptic setup could be \nquite complex, as there may be an additional offshore low \npressure system that could affect the approaching system from \nthe west. Regardless, mid to late next week is likely to be the\nbest chance of rain in the last three weeks. The system moves\nthrough and out of the area by next weekend.",
